PHASE 6 — EXPAND ALL ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING CONTENT

Extend the electronics curriculum to include every topic in the project’s current electronics syllabus and any listed but unimplemented engineering subject.

Cover:

1. Mathematics and physics for electronics.
2. Electrical fundamentals.
3. Passive components.
4. Semiconductor physics and devices.
5. Analog electronics.
6. Digital electronics.
7. Power supplies and power electronics.
8. Measurement and instrumentation.
9. Microcontrollers and embedded systems.
10. PCB design and manufacturing.
11. Communications and signal processing.
12. Advanced electronics engineering.
13. Practical repair and troubleshooting.

Every complex topic must be explained separately and in appropriate depth.

Examples of required expansion:

Electrical fundamentals

Develop separate instructional sections for Voltage, Current, Resistance, Conductance, Ohm’s law, Power, Energy, Series circuits, Parallel circuits, Kirchhoff’s laws, Voltage dividers, Current dividers, DC and AC, RMS, and measurement uncertainty.

Include derivations, calculations, circuit diagrams, assumptions and practical measurements.

Passive components

Teach resistor colour codes, resistor tolerance, power dissipation, capacitor charging and discharging, ESR, leakage, inductive reactance, RL circuits, RLC resonance and inductor saturation in sufficient detail.

Include actual numerical examples and identify datasheet-dependent properties.

Semiconductor devices

Teach PN-junction behaviour, diode I–V curves, rectification, Zener regulation, BJT operating regions, transistor biasing, MOSFET operating regions, gate-drive requirements, switching losses and safe operating area.

Do not present a typical component parameter as a universal device constant.

Analog electronics

Develop detailed material on operational amplifier assumptions, feedback, amplifier gain, bandwidth, slew rate, saturation, offset, CMRR, PSRR, filters, oscillators and stability.

Include ideal calculations and explain how real-device limitations change the result.

Digital electronics

Include truth tables, Boolean derivations, combinational circuits, latches, flip-flops, counters, setup and hold time, propagation delay, metastability and finite-state machines.

Power electronics

Explain rectification, filtering, linear regulators, switching converters, PWM, duty cycle, inductor ripple, output ripple, feedback, efficiency, switching losses, thermal design and EMI.

Label ideal equations and practical approximations correctly.

Instrumentation and multimeter design

Expand the development of a practical low-voltage multimeter, including voltage dividers, ADC resolution, input impedance, reference voltage, analog front ends, input protection, autoranging, calibration, noise, measurement uncertainty and testing.

Distinguish a conceptual design from a validated hardware design.

Embedded systems

Teach GPIO, timers, PWM, ADC, DAC, UART, SPI, I2C, DMA, interrupts, clock configuration, embedded C, firmware debugging and hardware interfacing.

Connect lessons to practical STM32 and other supported microcontroller projects.

PCB design

Teach schematics, symbols, footprints, netlists, grounding, decoupling, trace width, current capacity, clearance, creepage, thermal management, EMC, design-rule checking and Gerber generation.

Repair and troubleshooting

Structure each case as:

Symptom → Possible causes → Diagnostic measurements → Hypothesis → Confirming test → Fault isolation → Repair → Verification.

Do not declare a component defective solely because a symptom resembles a known failure.

